  "opening": "In the preceding chapters, we used single-qubit gates, CNOT gates, and reversible oracle boxes to build quantum computations. On an actual quantum computer, however, any multi-qubit unitary transformation must be constructed step by step from a small set of elementary gates.",
  "opening_continuation": "We first set out the rules for reading and composing multi-qubit circuit diagrams and build standard gate families. We then ask which gate sets can construct any unitary operation and how to count the resources a circuit requires.",
  "intro_continuation": "Can a small, fixed collection of one- and two-qubit gates build—or closely approximate—every \\(n\\)-qubit unitary matrix? And if so, how do we diagram, assemble, and measure the cost of the resulting circuits?"
}
